claims.statement:
Explicit form of the g^1 mu^9 gamma sector (23 derivatives):
{I^{x12}}_0|_{g mu^9 gamma} = (-i/64 pi^{11/2})^2 * 12! * sum_pqr |Aut|^{-1} * sum_{40 flows f} * sum_{12288 letter maps pi} s_pi (D_theta(lambda)/2) * int_{Delta^2} d^2w prod_P w_P^{11/2-n_P} * int dx N_pi(y(x,w)) applied to prod_{v interior} X_v.
Here |Aut| = 4,2,2,4,4 for 166,256,346,355,445 and X_v is the external letter at v. The currents are y^P_j = w_P sum_{P'} m_{P'} - m_P + Q^P_j (S3 eq-series and eq-parallel with lambda_N = 0). s_pi is the Grassmann sign of Delta_{e_1}...Delta_{e_13} acting on W_{v_0}...W_{v_11}. N_pi is the contraction of the vertex tensors, with J_e the current in the flow direction:
- pass-through: mu_v^j J_{e,i} - delta^j_i (J_e . mu_v);
- trivalent b_0: delta^j_{i''} J_{e,i'} - delta^j_{i'} J_{e,i''};
- source: lambda_{v,i'} gamma_{v,i''} - lambda_{v,i''} gamma_{v,i'};
- (c): (g/6) eps^{ijklm}, with each derivative giving J on a contracted letter or lambda_v on the external one.

ranking.rationale:
New results:
- the all-loop (H,T)=(1,1) fan kernel K_n = (4 pi lambda_1 lambda_{n+1})^{n/2}/((n+1)(n!)^2);
- the general-source formula as a Beta-Dirichlet expectation of a hafnian;
- all-order Taylor coefficients;
- the Gaussian-source lemma, which writes the T=1, H=1 integrand as a Gaussian average over sources of the (2,0) integrand.
None of this is in GKW, which gives only H=T=1 integrands, or in 2611.23377 (one loop).
Penalties:
- the method is S1's fan reduction, and S1 Sec. 10 announces the HT extension;
- the (2,0) control reproduces S1 eq-1-41;
- holomorphic gauge is one-loop exact for 3d HT CS (Gwilliam-Williams 2111.00955; Garner-Williams-Balduf 2611.23377), so the physical, gauge-invariant content of heat-kernel two-loop fans at (1,1) is not established;
- a10 (K_6, K_8) is unverified;
- the Jacobian sign of the chart is proved only for n=2,4.

No loop-corrected supercharge in twisted SYM outside 4d was found. Budzik-Kulp 2512.07771 is 4d one loop, and S1 is 4d.
New: Q_3 of hCS on C^{3|2}. It has 5 graphs, p=1 is forced, and {Q_0,Q_3}+{Q_1,Q_2}=0 has a unique solution with |kappa|=1/|Aut|. All 10 claims are confirmed with an operator engine that shares no code with the explorer's, on new operators and on so(6).
Penalties:
- it is a direct continuation of the unpublished S2 note, so it is novel only if S2 is published with it or first;
- the Koszul signs are fitted by nilpotency inside a 5-graph ansatz;
- the operator identity is tested on finitely many functionals;
- the 6d theory is non-renormalizable.

New as stated: closed forms for every tight theta kernel at T=0 and T=1, for all H, with general sources and an integral representation at z != 0. All 15 claims are verified, including direct numerics at z != 0.
Penalties:
- GKW sec:projective already shows the H=T=1 bitriangle density depends on t only through U^{-5/2}, with source factor lambda_1(lambda_1+lambda_2+lambda_3); the general-H statement D = +-D_theta/(2U^{H+3/2}) extends this;
- rho_2 is the published Pfaffian form (Balduf-Hu 2503.09558 Thm 1; BHP Def. 2.1; Balduf-Gaiotto 2408.03192);
- the H=2 values are in S1 and the operatope paper (1/24, f_bitriangle);
- the values follow by one Dirichlet integration once the density is known;
- H=3 and H=5 overlap the unpublished S2 and S3 notes;
- C13 and C14 (Monte Carlo diagnosis) concern an internal script and are not literature-level results.

The 8d twist (holomorphic BF on C^4) is known (ESW 2002.10517, 6.1.1). The pentagon Q_1 is the d=4 analogue of the known one-loop (d+1)-gon.
New: closed theta kernels on C^4, including the three-chain theta(3,3,3), and the inhomogeneous check Q_1^2+{Q_0,Q_2}=0 for su(2). That check has 24064 equations, rank 3, and unique (2,-4,-2/3) = 4/|Aut| up to sign.
Penalties:
- C1, C3, C10, C13 are unverified (normalization, eps_W);
- the signs of k are fitted;
- only su(2) was tested;
- the C12 fit is invariant under Q_n -> beta^n Q_n, so it tests only the |Aut| factors.

New: the pi-power theorem K in pi^{ceil(n/2)+2E} Q[lambda] (conditional on an unproved exponent hypothesis); the Stokes vanishing of the [2,2,2,4,4,4] kernel for all lambda, z and insertions; and the super-Gaussian merge recursion.
Already published, or immediate:
- rho^rho = 0 (Balduf-Hu Cor. 4(iv); Balduf-Gaiotto Thm 2; observed in GKW);
- the Berezin form, which is the Gaussian/Pfaffian identity;
- the banana rho product formula (BHP Lemma 2.4, dipole pullback).
Refuted: a5 (K_banana has the wrong sign for odd H, including theta(1,2,2) at H=1) and a6. c5 is open.

Already published:
- the twist reductions (ESW 5.1.1, 7.1.1, 9.1.1, 11.1.1);
- Q_odd = 0 at T=1 (GKW sec:projective; WW Prop. 3.23; Balduf-Gaiotto Lemma 4; Balduf-Hu Lemma 3(ii));
- the theta-graph content, which follows from the GKW counting.
The kernels are cases of twoloop-universal.
New: the 5d Q_2 operator with closure null space (1,1), the 7d ratio (-3,6,1), and the 3d single-graph closure.
Penalties:
- raw-normalization, Q2-5d-operator and S2-Bnorm-sign are unverified;
- the closure test is homogeneous (Q_1=0), so it fixes only ratios;
- 7d is in the arm convention and so(3) only.

flagship_statements:
Notation.
Reduced kernel: K_Gamma(lambda;z) = int_{RP^{|I|-1}_{>0}} e^{-sum_e y_e.z_e} U^{-H} rho_n^T wedge prod_{i,r} psi^i_r, with rho_n = det(M)^{-1/2} Pf(-1/2 dM M^{-1} dM) (Balduf-Hu 2503.09558; Brown-Hu-Panzer 2406.12734). Change of cycle basis: K -> det(A)^{H+T} K.
Theta graph theta(p,q,r):
- trivalent vertices N and N' = root;
- paths P in {A,B,C} with n_P = p,q,r edges P_1..P_{n_P}, oriented N -> N';
- edge order A,B,C; cycle matrix C3 = (A-B, B-C);
- interior vertex P_k between P_k and P_{k+1}, with source lambda^P_k; Lambda_P = [lambda^P_1..lambda^P_{n_P-1}] in Mat_{H x (n_P-1)};
- tightness: p+q+r = 2(H+T)+1 and n_P <= H+T.
Further definitions:
- R_P = sum_j t_{P_j}; U = R_A R_B + R_B R_C + R_C R_A;
- sigma^P_k = sum_{j>k} t_{P_j}/R_P; L(t) = lambda_N + sum_{P,k} sigma^P_k lambda^P_k;
- D_theta = det[(Lambda_A;0),(Lambda_B;Lambda_B),(0;Lambda_C)];
- Q(v) = det[(v;0),(0;v),(Lambda_A;0),(Lambda_B;Lambda_B),(0;Lambda_C)] (2H x 2H, columns written as (top block; bottom block)).

Theorem 1 (theta kernels, T=1, all H >= 0).
With respect to iota_E prod dt_e, the Schwinger density is D(t) = (-1)^{H(H+1)/2} D_theta/(2 U^{H+3/2}). It depends on t only through U and does not depend on lambda_N.
K[C3](lambda;0) = (-1)^{H(H+1)/2} (D_theta/2) prod_P Gamma(H+3/2-n_P) / (Gamma(H+3/2) prod_P (n_P-1)!).
In the positively oriented basis (det[B^T;C] > 0), K = (-1)^{(q+1)(H+1)} K[C3].
Values of K[C3]/(pi D_theta) (signs (-1)^{H(H+1)/2} included; the 1/2 of the formula is absorbed):
- H=0, (1,1,1): 1/2;
- H=1, (1,2,2): -1/3;
- H=2, (1,3,3), (2,2,3): -1/20, -1/30;
- H=3, (1,4,4), (2,3,4), (3,3,3): 1/252, 1/420, 1/840;
- H=4, (1,5,5), (2,4,5), (3,3,5), (3,4,4): 1/5184, 1/9072, 1/10080, 1/22680;
- H=5, (1,6,6), (2,5,6), (3,4,6), (3,5,5), (4,4,5): -1/158400, -1/285120, -1/332640, -1/798336, -1/997920.
(The twoloop-universal digest lists |c1| = 1 at H=0; the value K = pi follows from the positive-basis sign and is consistent with the 1/2 above.)
Root independence: with lambda_root = -sum_{v != root} lambda_v, K[C3] does not depend on the root. At T=1, D_theta depends on lambda_N and lambda_N' only through lambda_N + lambda_N'.
Proved; checked exactly and pointwise on all 16 tight graphs with H <= 5.

Proposition 3 (theta kernels at z != 0).
Definitions: Z_P = sum_j z_{P_j}; w_P = R_P^{-1}/sum_Q R_Q^{-1}; W = sum_P w_P Z_P; x^P_j = t_{P_j}/R_P.
Displacement map: -sum_e y_e.z_e = sum_v lambda_v.X_v, with
- X_N = -W;
- X_{P_k} = sigma^P_k (Z_P - W) - sum_{j>k} z_{P_j};
- X_{N'} = 0.
Then
- K^{T=1}[C3](lambda;z) = (-1)^{H(H+1)/2} (D_theta/2) int_{Delta^2} d^2w prod_P w_P^{H+1/2-n_P} int_{prod_P Delta^{n_P-1}} dx e^{sum_v lambda_v.X_v};
- K^{T=0}[C3](lambda;z) = (-1)^{H(H+1)/2+1} int d^2w prod_P w_P^{H-n_P} int dx Q(L(x)) e^{sum_v lambda_v.X_v}.
Verified pointwise, by 55 moments, and by direct numerical integration at z != 0 (13-15 digits).

Theorem 7 (zeta(3) on C^3, n=3).
Conventions: H=3, T=0; edges as listed, oriented from smaller to larger label; positive cycle basis. Sources e1, e2, e3, -(e1+e2+e3) sit at the degree-two vertices d1<d2<d3<d4, all others zero, and Delta = det(lambda_{d1}, lambda_{d2}, lambda_{d3}). Then K(lambda;0) = c_Gamma Delta^3 with:
- [1,1,1,1,3,3], edges (0,4)(0,7)(1,2)(1,5)(1,7)(2,3)(2,4)(3,6)(4,5)(5,6): c = (39-32 zeta(3))/67200;
- [1,1,1,2,2,3]a, edges (0,4)(0,7)(1,2)(1,4)(1,5)(2,3)(2,6)(3,4)(5,6)(5,7): c = -1/77760;
- [1,1,1,2,2,3]b, edges (0,4)(0,7)(1,2)(1,4)(1,5)(2,3)(2,6)(3,4)(5,6)(6,7): c = (1296 zeta(3)-1597)/5443200;
- [1,1,1,2,2,3]c, edges (0,4)(0,7)(1,2)(1,5)(2,3)(2,4)(3,6)(3,7)(4,5)(5,6): c = (34-27 zeta(3))/453600;
- [1,1,2,2,2,2]a, edges (0,1)(0,4)(0,7)(1,2)(2,3)(2,5)(3,6)(3,7)(4,5)(5,6): c = 0 (odd automorphism (3 5)(4 7));
- [1,1,2,2,2,2]b, edges (0,1)(0,5)(0,6)(1,2)(1,7)(2,3)(3,4)(3,6)(4,5)(4,7): c = (39-32 zeta(3))/67200.
Status: computer-assisted exact evaluation, with the sourced star-triangle chart and two centres agreeing. Independent reduction-free integration agrees to relative <= 2.5e-7.

Theorem 8 (zeta(3) on C^2, prism, n=4).
Prism edges (0,1)(0,2)(0,3)(1,4)(1,5)(2,3)(2,5)(3,4)(4,5), H=2, T=0. Sources lambda_0, lambda_1, lambda_2 generic, lambda_3 = -(lambda_0+lambda_1+lambda_2), lambda_4 = lambda_5 = 0. Write K(lambda;0) = sum c_ijk <01>^i <02>^j <12>^k.
Nonzero coefficients:
- c_400 = -1/34560;
- c_310 = 79/134400 - 3 zeta(3)/5600;
- c_301 = 1667/1209600 - 3 zeta(3)/2800;
- c_220 = -79/134400 + 3 zeta(3)/5600;
- c_211 = 19/16800 - zeta(3)/1400;
- c_202 = -193/403200 + zeta(3)/2800;
- c_130 = -1/11520;
- c_121 = 41/44800 - 3 zeta(3)/2800;
- c_112 = -29/134400 - zeta(3)/2800;
- c_103 = 299/1209600 - zeta(3)/2800.
All c_0jk = 0.
Rational cases:
- three sources at {0,1,2}: K = <lambda_0,lambda_1>^4/34560, reached by a source-free reduction;
- the C^2 non-series-parallel graph at n=3 (K4 with edge 01 subdivided): rational with four sources, K = -(p^3+p^2q-3p^2s-5pq^2-12pqs-3ps^2)/576;
- K_{3,3} vanishes whenever two source-free vertices lie on one side.

risks:
Published overlap:
- rho_n = det^{-1/2} Pf(-1/2 dM M^{-1} dM) is Balduf-Hu 2503.09558 Thm 1 / Def. 2 and Brown-Hu-Panzer 2406.12734 Def. 2.1 (the exact check rho_2 = pi phi_{D_3} was run);
- rho^rho = 0: BH Cor. 4(iv), Balduf-Gaiotto Thm 2;
- odd-loop vanishing at T=1: GKW, WW Prop. 3.23, BG Lemma 4;
- the H=T=1 bitriangle density ~ U^{-5/2} lambda_1(lambda_1+lambda_2+lambda_3): GKW sec:projective;
- the banana rho product: BHP Lemma 2.4.
Presenting any of these as new would be an error.

Sign conventions are fragile:
- a5 (banana K formula) was refuted by a sign error for odd H;
- the a2 rho_n formula flips sign between the unnormalized and positively oriented bases for n = 2, 6;
- S1 Fig. 3.1 uses an edge order with the opposite two-loop sign;
- S2 eq-B-norm differs by (-1)^n from the derived normalization at d = 3, 4.
Any theorem stated with a sign must specify the edge order, the orientation and the cycle-basis convention.

ranking.rationale:
Confirmed: a1, b1-b4, c1, c4-c8. Unverified: c2 (normalization hbar^2/(64 pi^5), varsigma, contraction sign) and c3 (assembly with sgn(L->L') and the odd-generator Wick relative sign). Robust part: F_133 = -F_223 mod exact, and [F_223] = 0 for sl2, gl2 and != 0 for sl_N, gl_N, N>=3. The verifier added a formal-in-N proof for all N>=4. Since |K_133| != |K_223|, any signs a, b of the two graph terms give a class (b-a)[F_223] != 0, so non-exactness does not depend on the unverified relative sign.

Unproved:
- the identification of local BRST cohomology with CE cochains of g[[z1,z2]] mod sum p (argued, not sourced);
- the freedom to change one-loop counterterms (a closed J_1 changing O_2), not analysed;
- the overall constant and sign of O_2;
- c8 upper bounds, which rest on sample stability and ranks mod two primes;
- a1 absolute convergence;
- b1: the assembly O_n = c_n int Omega Delta^n K A^4 with 1/|Aut| is not from a source equation, and c_n and the H=3 Koszul sign are unfixed;
- b2 step 5 (H^4(s)=0) is cited and checked only for sl2, sl3, sl4, so4, so5.

Assumption: no counterterms below order n. b4 shows the one-loop box is non-exact, so the hCS on C^3 statement covers only classical-vertex graphs. Sign remark: S2 eq-IA in S2's labelling gives -Delta^2/432 on sum p = 0.

10/10 confirmed. The verifier's operator engine shares no code with opcheck.py or S2 salg.py. It was validated on S2 at n=1,2 and gives the unique kappa=(-1/2,1/2,-1,1/8,-1/2) on new operators in so(5) and so(6). Unproved:
- the Koszul sign of W_Gamma relative to I_Gamma (the sigma_Gamma rule is fitted on box, A, B, G1-G5; 48 relabellings test invariance only);
- kappa is unique only inside the 5-graph ansatz;
- nilpotency is tested on finitely many operators and random plane-wave functionals, not by full expansion;
- the theta-translation explanation of C10;
- S2 (5.11) and (5.13) are inputs, not re-derived.
Unchecked or erroneous details: eps_T det B_T for cotree [0,6,4] depends on cotree order (G2 +1, G4 -1 in S2 order). The htk values quoted for G4 and G5 are I = -int J. The overall sign of kappa is tied to the sign of Q_1 in S2 (1.4). The Q_0, Q_1, Q_2 normalizations come from the S2 text, while the ops.py docstring disagrees. Assumptions from S2 Section 8.2: UV finiteness, Ward identity, twisting commutes with quantization, a non-renormalizable 6d theory, no instantons.
